\use{document}
\use{music}

\title{The \mono{music} Package}
\author{Engraved staff notation, drawn from the document language}
\maketitle

\section{Reading a score}

The \mono{music} package draws a line of staff notation. A score, written
\mono{\\score\{\ldots\}}, is a space-separated list of notes. A note is an
optional accidental, a letter \mono{a}–\mono{g} for the pitch, optional
\mono{'} or \mono{,} to raise or lower it an octave, an optional duration
digit, an optional augmentation dot, and any trailing marks. A \mono{|} is a
barline and \mono{r} a rest. The heads, clefs, flags, rests, accidentals and
time-signature figures are real engraved glyphs from a SMuFL music font; the
stems, beams, staff lines, ledger lines and barlines are ruled over the
\mono{\\picture} layer.

\section{A scale}

The C major scale, an octave of quarter notes from middle C — which sits on its
own ledger line below the staff — up to the C above. The quarter note is the
default, so it needs no duration digit.

\bigskip
\centerline{\score{c d e f g a b c'}}

\section{Durations}

The same C, held for a whole note, a half, a quarter, an eighth, a sixteenth,
and a thirty-second. The longer notes have open heads; the stem appears at the
half note, a flag at the eighth, a second flag at the sixteenth, and a third at
the thirty-second.

\bigskip
\centerline{\score{c1 c2 c4 c8 c16 c32}}

\section{Rests}

A \mono{r} is a rest, sized to its duration the same way a note is. Here a
whole rest, a half, a quarter, and an eighth, each between sounding notes.

\bigskip
\centerline{\score{c r1 d r2 e r4 f r8 g}}

\section{Dots}

A dot after the duration lengthens a note by half. It is drawn just right of the
head, in the space above when the note sits on a line. Here a dotted half, then a
dotted quarter paired with the eighth that completes its beat.

\bigskip
\centerline{\score{c2. d4 | e4. e8 f4 g4}}

\section{Range and ledger lines}

Notes far above or below the staff hang on short ledger lines, drawn only as far
as the note reaches. Here a span from two octaves below middle C up past the top
of the staff.

\bigskip
\centerline{\score{c, e, g, c g c' e' g'}}

\section{Accidentals}

An accidental leads a note: \mono{+} for a sharp, \mono{-} for a flat,
\mono{n} for a natural. The sign is drawn just left of the head; it changes how
the note is read, not where it sits, so \mono{+f} and \mono{f} share a line.
Here a chromatic climb, then a sharpened note cancelled by a natural.

\bigskip
\centerline{\score{c +c d -e e f +f g | +f nf f2}}

\section{Beams}

Eighth notes in a run are usually joined by a beam rather than each carrying its
own flag. Bracket a group with \mono{[} and \mono{]} and the notes between
share one beam.

\bigskip
\centerline{\score{[ c d e f ] [ g a b c' ]}}

\bigskip
The beam is not flat: it tilts to follow the line of the melody, clamped so a
wide leap does not run it off the staff. Here a rising group, a falling one, and
an arch that the beam clears.

\bigskip
\centerline{\score{[ c d e f g ] [ g f e d c ] [ c e g e c ]}}

\subsection{Double beams}

A run of sixteenths carries a second beam below the first, and a run of
thirty-seconds a third. The bracket beams whatever durations it contains.

\bigskip
\centerline{\score{[ c16 d16 e16 f16 ] [ g32 a32 b32 c'32 ] [ c8 d16 e16 f8 ]}}

\section{Time signatures}

Setting \mono{\\set musictimenum} and \mono{\\set musictimeden} draws a time
signature after the clef. Three-four time, a waltz, with the beats grouped into
bars.

\bigskip
\set musictimenum {3}
\set musictimeden {4}
\centerline{\score{c e g | c' g e | d f a | g2 e}}
\set musictimenum {}

\bigskip
A two-digit signature, twelve-eight, stays centred as a block.

\bigskip
\set musictimenum {12}
\set musictimeden {8}
\centerline{\score{[ c8 d e ] [ e f g ] | [ g a b ] [ b a g ]}}
\set musictimenum {}
\set musictimeden {}

\section{Key signatures}

A key signature follows the clef. Set \mono{\\set musickey} to a signed count: a
positive number draws that many sharps, a negative one that many flats, each in
its conventional order and on its conventional line. Here two sharps (D major),
then three flats (E-flat major).

\bigskip
\set musickey {2}
\set musictimenum {3}
\set musictimeden {4}
\centerline{\score{d e +f g | a b +c' d' | b a g2}}
\set musictimenum {}
\set musictimeden {}
\bigskip
\set musickey {-3}
\centerline{\score{c d e f g a b c'}}
\set musickey {0}

\section{Repeats and barlines}

Besides the plain barline \mono{|}, a score can write a double barline
\mono{||}, a final barline \mono{|.}, and the start and end of a repeated
section, \mono{|:} and \mono{:|}.

\bigskip
\centerline{\score{|: c d e f | g a b c' :| d' c' b a || g1 |.}}

\section{Articulations}

A mark after a note tells the player how to attack it: \mono{>} an accent,
\mono{!} a staccato, \mono{=} a tenuto, \mono{*} a marcato, \mono{;} a
fermata. Each sits on the side away from the stem, and several may stack. Here
the same phrase plain, then accented, staccato, tenuto and marcato, closing on a
note that is both marcato and held under a fermata.

\bigskip
\centerline{\score{c d e f | c> d> e> f> | c! d! e! f! | c= d= e= f= | g2*;}}

\section{Slurs and ties}

A run bracketed by \mono{(} and \mono{)} is drawn under one slur. A trailing
\mono{-} ties a note to the next of the same pitch, holding it across. Here a
slurred turn, then two tied half notes.

\bigskip
\centerline{\score{( c d e f g ) a | c'-2 c'2}}

\section{Dynamics and hairpins}

A token beginning \mono{!} is a dynamic, set below the staff: \mono{!p},
\mono{!mf}, \mono{!ff}, \mono{!sfz}. A \mono{<} opens a crescendo hairpin
and \mono{>} a diminuendo, each closed by \mono{=}. Here a phrase that starts
soft, swells, and falls away.

\bigskip
\centerline{\score{!p c d < e f g a = !f b c' > b a g f = !p e2}}

\section{The bass clef}

Setting \mono{\\set musicclef \{bass\}} notates in the bass clef instead, where
middle C sits one ledger line \italic{above} the staff. Here a C major scale from
the C below the staff up to middle C.

\set musicclef {bass}
\bigskip
\centerline{\score{c, d, e, f, g, a, b, c}}

\section{Alto and tenor clefs}

The C clef names the line that is middle C: the middle line in the \mono{alto}
clef, the fourth line in the \mono{tenor}. The same C major scale around middle
C, read in each.

\set musicclef {alto}
\bigskip
\centerline{\score{c d e f g a b c'}}

\set musicclef {tenor}
\bigskip
\centerline{\score{c d e f g a b c'}}

\section{A short piece}

Everything together: the opening of \italic{Ode to Joy} in the treble clef, in
common time, with beamed eighths, a slurred phrase, a dynamic, and a final
barline to close.

\set musicclef {treble}
\set musictimenum {4}
\set musictimeden {4}
\bigskip
\centerline{\score{!mf e e f g | g f e d | c c d e | ( e4. d8 ) d2 |.}}
\set musictimenum {}
\set musictimeden {}

\section{Choosing the music font}

The glyphs come from a SMuFL music font, and \mono{\\set musicfont} chooses which
one. The default is Bravura, the standard reference font; \mono{petaluma} is a
handwritten, jazz-lead-sheet alternative — the same notes, a different hand. Here
one phrase set in each.

\bigskip
\centerline{\score{[ c8 d e f ] g2 a4 | b4 c'2 r4 | g4*; }}

\set musicfont {petaluma}
\bigskip
\centerline{\score{[ c8 d e f ] g2 a4 | b4 c'2 r4 | g4*; }}
\set musicfont {bravura}
